# Week 6 — Conceptual Domain Modelling (UML Class Diagrams)


## Part A — Review of Earlier Work

### Task A1: Requirement Evidence Identification
1. Which requirement tells us that Booking is an important concept
   A student can make more than one equipment booking.
2. Which requirement identifies two attributes of Booking
   Each booking records a start date and end date
3. Which requirements indicate relationships involving Staff Member
   Staff members review booking requests
   Staff members manage equipment availability


## Part B — Identify Domain Concepts

### Task B1: Candidate Concept Table

| Candidate | Class, Attribute, or Action? | Supporting Requirement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Student | Class | FR01 |
| Booking | Class | FR01, FR02, FR03 |
| Start date | Attribute | FR03 |
| End date | Attribute | FR03 |
| Equipment | Class | FR01, FR02, FR05 |
| Review | Action | FR04 |
| Staff Member | Class | FR04, FR05 |


### Task B2: Core Domain Classes Justification
1. Student: Represents the primary user who requests and holds equipment reservations within the system
2. Booking: Represents the central transactional domain record containing reservation dates, status, and item associations
3. Equipment: Represents the tangible resource being reserved and managed for availability
4. Staff Member: Represents the administrative role responsible for reviewing requests and overseeing equipment availability


### Task B3: Identification of Uncertainty
Candidate Attribute: emailAddress for Student or status for Booking
Uncertainty: While recording an email or booking status is logically useful, FR01–FR06 do not explicitly state how notifications or approval states are stored.
Target Stakeholder: College Facilities Manager / System Administrator


## Part E — Multiplicity & Open Questions

### Task E2 & E3: Multiplicity Analysis & Open Questions

#### OPEN QUESTIONS Note
1. Student — Booking: Can a booking be made jointly on behalf of multiple students, or is it strictly tied to exactly one student
2. Booking — Equipment: How many historical or future bookings can reference the same equipment item over time (`0..*`)
3. Staff Member — Booking: Is each booking reviewed by exactly one assigned staff member, or can multiple staff members review the same request
4. Staff Member — Equipment: Can multiple staff members manage the same equipment item, or is equipment divided among specific staff


